<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n31657" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">ob-lăquĕo</span></b> ,
			āre, v. a.
		<div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b>
			<i>To dig about the roots</i> of trees, etc. (cf.
			ablaqueo): 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">arbores oblaqueatae sunt,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Col.
					2, 14, 3</span></i>
			; <i><span class="en">Isid. Orig. 17, 5,
			31</span></i>.—</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>II.</b>
			<i>To surround, encircle, set</i> (eccl. Lat.): 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">argento gemmas oblaqueare,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Tert. Res. Carn. 7</span></i>
			.</div>
    </div> <div class="alpheios-lex-footnotes en"> </div>
    <div class="alpheios-lex-alph-source">From <u>
            <u>A Latin Dictionary</u>
        </u> (Charlton T. Lewis, Charles Short, Oxford, Clarendon Press, 1879) </div>
</div>